/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (4.     The Information Technology Act, 2000 was India's first comprehensive law governing cybercrimes, electronic transactions, and online communication. This legislation provided legal recognition to electronic contracts, records, and signatures, and criminalized various forms of cybercrime such as hacking and data breaches.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (5.     Despite its progressive nature, the IT Act, 2000 had several loopholes and procedural challenges. The Act has undergone amendments, notably in 2008, to adapt to the evolving challenges of the internet era. The 2008 amendment significantly expanded the scope of the IT Act to address emerging cyber threats, including cyber terrorism, phishing, identity theft, and, most notably, crimes that violate the safety and dignity of women in online spaces. This amendment introduced stricter penalties and new provisions to tackle cyber stalking, voyeurism, the transmission of sexually explicit material, and the non-consensual distribution of private images. These changes empowered law enforcement agencies with more robust legal tools to combat digital crimes that disproportionately affect women.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
